---
/**
 * The desktop wallpaper: Bliss, the Windows XP one.
 *
 * PROVENANCE. It is Charles O'Rear's photograph, owned by Microsoft. The file
 * comes from the bg.jpg of winbows.neocities.org (a 3840x2160 remaster),
 * rescaled here to 2560x1440 and re-encoded to WebP q72: 1008 KB -> 225 KB.
 * This contradicts §3 of the spec, which said to draw it so as not to touch
 * anyone else's work; it was changed at the express request of the repo's
 * owner, who wanted the real wallpaper. The credit is in the README, next to
 * the licensing caveat.
 *
 * As a file and not inline: 225 KB of base64 inside the HTML would be 300 KB of
 * uncacheable HTML on every visit. The flash while it loads is covered by the
 * fallback gradient below, and on a first visit the boot screen covers all of
 * it.
 */
---

<img
  class="xp-wallpaper"
  src="/xp/bliss.webp"
  width="2560"
  height="1440"
  alt=""
  aria-hidden="true"
  fetchpriority="high"
  decoding="async"
/>

<style>
  .xp-wallpaper {
    position: fixed;
    inset: 0;
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    /* `cover` crops instead of distorting, like a real wallpaper (§6). The
       width/height attributes are the file's own and only exist so the browser
       reserves the box; the CSS decides. */
    object-fit: cover;
    /* A reserve in case the image never arrives: sky at the top, grass at the
       bottom, split where the horizon falls. A failure looks like a poor
       landscape, not like a blank screen. */
    background: linear-gradient(#4b8fd4 0 50%, #6aa32b 50%, #4e7409);
    z-index: 0;
  }
</style>
